</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/04:_Unit_Four/4.01:_Day_27-_Thermochemistry_and_Enthalpy" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="D27.6_Hess.E2.80.99s_Law"></span><h2 class="editable">D27.6 Hess&rsquo;s Law</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">The enthalpy change for a particular reaction is the same regardless of which or how many steps the reaction is carried out in. This conveniently allows us to calculate the heat transfer for a chemical change from other experimentally determined enthalpy changes. This type of calculation usually involves <strong>Hess&rsquo;s law</strong>: <em>If a process can be written as the sum of several stepwise processes, the enthalpy change of the total process equals the sum of the enthalpy changes of the various steps</em>.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">For example, we can think of this reaction occurring in a single step:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371697">C(<em>s</em>) + O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &#10230; CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -394 kJ/mol</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">or in a two-step process:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">step 1:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:right;">C(<em>s</em>) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">CO(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -111 kJ/mol</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">step 2:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:right;">CO(<em>g</em>) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -283 kJ/mol</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">According to Hess&rsquo;s law, the &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of the one-step reaction equals the sum of the &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of the two steps in the two-step reaction:</p>

<table class="mt-responsive-table" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto;border:0px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">step 1:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:right;">C(<em>s</em>) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">CO(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -111 kJ/mol</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid">
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">step 2:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:right;">CO(<em>g</em>) + &frac12;O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -283 kJ/mol</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">Sum:</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:right;">C(<em>s</em>) + O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&#10230;</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:left;">CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>)</td>
            <td class="mt-noheading lt-chem-371697" style="text-align:center;">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -394 kJ/mol</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">This concept is illustrated in Figure 4.</p>

<figure><img alt="An enthalpy diagram is shown. A long arrow faces upward on the left with the phrase &ldquo;H increasing.&rdquo; A horizontal line at the bottom of the diagram is shown with the formula &ldquo;C O subscript 2 (g)&rdquo; below it. A horizontal line at the top of the diagram has the formulas &ldquo;C (s) + O subscript 2 (g)&rdquo; above it. The top and bottom lines are connected by a downward facing arrow with the value &ldquo;&Delta; H = &ndash;394 k J&rdquo; written beside it. Below and to the right of the top horizontal line is a second horizontal line with the equations &ldquo;C O (g) + one half O subscript 2 (g)&rdquo; above it. This line and the bottom line are connected by a downward facing arrow with the value &ldquo;&Delta; H = &ndash;283 k J&rdquo; written beside it. The same line and the top line are connected by a downward facing arrow with the value &ldquo;&Delta; H = &ndash;111 k J&rdquo; written beside it. There are three brackets to the right of the diagram. The first bracket runs from the top horizontal line to the second horizontal line. It is labeled, &ldquo;Enthalpy of reactants.&rdquo; The second bracket runs from the second horizontal line to the bottom horizontal line. It is labeled, &ldquo;Enthalpy of products.&rdquo; Both of these brackets are included in the third bracket which runs from the top to the bottom of the diagram. It is labeled, &ldquo;Enthalpy change of exothermic reaction in 1 or 2 steps." class="internal default" style="width: 691px; height: 351px;" width="691px" height="351px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/402998/Hess_law.png?revision=1&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=691&amp;height=351" />
<figcaption><em><strong>Figure 4</strong>. Enthalpy values based on one mole of reaction: C(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) &#10230; CO<sub>2</sub>(g). The formation of CO<sub>2</sub>(g) from its elements can be thought of as occurring in two steps, which sum to the overall reaction, as described by Hess&rsquo;s law. For an exothermic process, the products are at lower enthalpy than the reactants.</em></figcaption>
</figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/04:_Unit_Four/4.01:_Day_27-_Thermochemistry_and_Enthalpy" class="mt-section" id="section_9"><span id="D27.7_Standard_Enthalpy_of_Formation"></span><h2 class="editable">D27.7 Standard Enthalpy of Formation</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-371697"><strong>Standard enthalpy of formation</strong>, <strong>&Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg;</strong>, is <em>the enthalpy change for a reaction in which exactly one mole of a pure substance in a specified state (s, l, or g) is formed from free elements in their most stable states under standard-state conditions</em>. &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; is also referred to as the <em>standard heat of formation</em>.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">For example, &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) at 25 &deg;C is &minus;393.5 kJ/mol. This is the enthalpy change for the exothermic reaction:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371697">C(<em>s</em>, graphite) + O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &#10230; CO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = -393.5 kJ/mol (25 &deg;C)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">The gaseous reactant and product are at a pressure of 1 bar, the carbon is present as graphite, which is the most stable form of carbon under standard conditions.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">For nitrogen dioxide, NO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>), &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; is 33.2 kJ/mol at 25 &deg;C:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371697">&frac12;N<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) + O<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &#10230; NO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>) &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = +33.2 kJ/mol (25 &deg;C)</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">A reaction equation with &frac12; mole of N<sub>2</sub> and 1 mole O<sub>2</sub> is appropriate in this case because the standard enthalpy of formation always refers to formation of 1 mol NO<sub>2</sub>(<em>g</em>).</p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">By definition, the standard enthalpy of formation of an element in its most stable form is 0 kJ/mol under standard conditions. A table of &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; values for many common substances can be found in the <a class="target-chapter" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" title="Under_Construction/Wisconsin_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/06%3A_Appendix/6.13%3A_Thermodynamic" href="/Under_Construction/Wisconsin_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/06%3A_Appendix/6.13%3A_Thermodynamic">Appendix</a>.</p>

<div class="box-query">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/04:_Unit_Four/4.01:_Day_27-_Thermochemistry_and_Enthalpy" class="mt-section"><span id="Exercise_9:_Equations_for_Standard_Formation_Enthalpy"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Exercise 9: Equations for Standard Formation Enthalpy</span></h5>



<iframe class="h5p-iframe" src="https://studio.libretexts.org/h5p/9220/embed" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen" width="100" allow="microphone"></iframe>

</div></div>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">Hess&rsquo;s law can be used to determine the &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of any reaction if the &Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of the reactants and products are available. In other words, we can think of any reaction as occurring via step-wise decomposition of the reactants into their component elements followed by re-combination of the elements to give the products. (Almost no reaction would actually occur via such a mechanism because some of the reactions would have large activation-energy barriers and consequently very small rates, but we can imagine such a path for the sole purpose of using Hess&rsquo;s law to calculate &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg;.<em>) </em></p>

<p class="lt-chem-371697">The &Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; of the overall reaction is therefore equal to:</p>

<p class="mt-align-center lt-chem-371697">&Delta;<sub>r</sub><em>H</em>&deg; = &sum;&Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg;(products) &ndash; &sum;&Delta;<sub>f</sub><em>H</em>&deg;(reactants)</p>

